<section id="sec-mistakes" class="col">
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul class="tight">
    <li><strong>Metres per minute in a power formula.</strong> \(P = m g v\) with \(v = 25\) gives about a megawatt, sixty times too much. The speed must be in m/s first. You catch it by the size of the answer. A tower crane does not carry a megawatt motor.</li>
    <li><strong>kN·m taken as a joule every time.</strong> Work is force times distance along the force. A moment is force times a lever arm across the force, and nothing moves. Both are N·m, only one is an energy. You catch it by asking what moved along the force. A hammer rating in kNm is an energy, because the ram moved. A bolt torque in N·m is not.</li>
    <li><strong>Faster hoist, more energy.</strong> The lift costs \(m g h\) at any speed. A faster hoist spends the same energy in less time, which is more power, not more energy. You catch it by the tiles. The energy tile does not move when you change the speed, the power tiles do.</li>
    <li><strong>Lowering for free.</strong> The cable’s work on a lowered load is \(-m g h\). The drive has to absorb that energy, in a brake resistor or by feeding it back, not deliver it. You catch it by the sign of \(P = T v\). The force points up and the speed is negative, so the power is negative.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

